    "mathematics": {
      "verdict": "revise",
      "independent_solution": "All legal solutions are((t+1)^2,t^2),t>=0; here(1,0),(4,1) are the two valid alternatives. This true set was sealed before reading the author. The author key agrees, but q39:b has the concrete internal misconception/derivation mismatch recorded below; it is not normalized to pass.",
      "correct": [
        "a",
        "c"
      ],
      "option_checks": [
        {
          "option_id": "a",
          "truth": "correct",
          "evidence": "sqrt1-sqrt0=1 and both radicands are nonnegative. Late internal check: Author legal t0 yields(1,0), matching principal-radical value1."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "b",
          "truth": "incorrect",
          "evidence": "sqrt2-1!=1; solving x-y=1 substitutes an invalid difference-of-roots rule. Late internal check: REVISE: derivation's false difference-of-squares relation x-y=1 with y1 does produce(2,1). However, the named misconception 'Omit the cross term when squaring a difference of square roots' instead gives x+y=1 after omitting-2sqrt(xy). At y1 that produces(0,1), not(2,1). The authored derivation and claimed misconception are not equivalent reproducible procedures. Displayed b remains false and independent correct set remains a,c."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "c",
          "truth": "correct",
          "evidence": "sqrt4-sqrt1=2-1=1 with legal radicands. Late internal check: Author legal t1 gives(4,1), matching principal-radical difference2-1=1."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "d",
          "truth": "incorrect",
          "evidence": "y=-1 is outside the real radical domain; sqrt(-1) is not a real principal root. Late internal check: Author applies false x-y=1, fixes x0,y-1, then omits original y>=0; exactly d. Its broad invalid-squaring plus missing-domain description matches that compound procedure."
        }
      ]
    }
  },
